SUMMARY:Ran | Michael A. McRobbie's Choice
DESCRIPTION:After years of consolidating his empire\, the elderly Great L
 ord Hidetora Ichimonji decides to abdicate and divide his domain amongst 
 his three sons. The eldest\, Taro\, will rule\, and his second and third 
 sons—Jiro and Saburo—will take command of his second and third castle
 s\, but are expected to obey their eldest brother. When Sabura is banishe
 d for disobedience\, the (literal) Shakespearean dramatics play out in Ku
 rosawa's third adaptation of the Bard. Widely considered one of the great
 est films ever made and among Kurosawa's finest filmmaking achievements\,
  Ran continues to set the bar in formally inventive and meticulously craf
 ted adaptive storytelling. [160 min\; tragedy\, drama\, war\; Japanese wi
 th English subtitles]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  After years of consolidating his empire
 \, the elderly Great Lord Hidetora Ichimonji decides to abdicate and divi
 de his domain amongst his three sons. The eldest\, Taro\, will rule\, and
  his second and third sons—Jiro and Saburo—will take command of his s
 econd and third castles\, but are expected to obey their eldest brother. 
 When Sabura is banished for disobedience\, the (literal) Shakespearean dr
 amatics play out in Kurosawa's third adaptation of the Bard. Widely consi
 dered one of the greatest films ever made and among Kurosawa's finest fil
 mmaking achievements\, <i>Ran</i> continues to set the bar in formally in
 ventive and meticulously crafted adaptive storytelling. [160 min\; traged
 y\, drama\, war\; Japanese with English subtitles]</p>
