SUMMARY:Journal for Audiovisual Ethnomusicology Shorts Program | Addition
 al Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:This screening is a public rental presented by the Journal fo
 r Audiovisual Ethnomusicology (JAVEM)\, a peer-reviewed journal dedicated
  to audiovisual scholarship in ethnomusicology and related fields. Togeth
 er\, the three films demonstrate the range of audiovisual ethnomusicology
  across generations: from Hugo Zemp's pioneering use of film as a tool fo
 r musical analysis\, to Richard K. Wolf's longitudinal and collaborative 
 engagement with Kota musical and ritual life\, to Matthew Machin-Autenrie
 th and Dario Ranocchiari's exploration of music\, history\, and cultural 
 memory in contemporary Granada.The Song of Harmonics/Le chant des harmoni
 ques(dir. Hugo Zemp\, 1989\, France): Made with ethnomusicologist and mus
 ician Trần Quang Hải\, Hugo Zemp's landmark film investigates overton
 e\, or biphonic\, singing\, a technique in which a single singer produces
  a low fundamental while selectively emphasizing its harmonics to create 
 a second audible melody. Moving between instruction\, Mongolian khöömii
  performance\, and scientific investigation\, the film uses spectral and 
 physiological imaging to make otherwise invisible aspects of vocal produc
 tion visible. The Song of Harmonics is also an important example of ethno
 musicological filmmaking in which audiovisual media function not simply t
 o document musical practice\, but as a method of research and analysis. T
 his screening is presented in tribute to Hugo Zemp (1937–2026)\, a foun
 dational figure in visual ethnomusicology\, who passed away on January 3\
 , 2026. [38 min\; documentary\; English and Mongolian]Resonant Histories:
  Echoes of Al-Andalus in Granada(dir. Matthew Machin-Autenrieth and Dario
  Ranocchiari\, 2026\, Spain):Resonant Histories traces the complex relati
 onship between Arab-Andalusian music and flamenco in Granada\, a city whe
 re encounters between North African and Spanish musicians have long shape
 d musical life. Traveling through the streets of Sacromonte\, closely ass
 ociated with flamenco\, and the Albaicín\, Granada's historic Arab quart
 er\, the filmmakers draw on musicians\, musicologists\, historians\, and 
 local enthusiasts to explore the ways the city's past continues to resona
 te in the present. The film moves from colonial exhibitions and early the
 atrical experiments combining Andalusí music and flamenco to Granada's c
 ontemporary music scene\, asking how histories of encounter\, exchange\, 
 and representation are remembered—and continually remade—through musi
 cal practice. [59 min\; documentary\; Spanish with English subtitles]Pots
  of Millet\, Faces of Gold: Transformation(dir. Richard K. Wolf\, 2025\, 
 India and USA): The first in a series of experimental ethnographic films 
 drawing on Richard K. Wolf's 35 years of research with the Kota people of
  South India's Nilgiri Hills\, Transformation explores a community of few
 er than 2\,500 people whose history has included disease\, forced migrati
 on\, and disruptions in the transmission of traditional knowledge. The fi
 lm's title evokes two poles of Kota ritual life: millet\, central to mult
 iday mortuary ceremonies\, and gold-colored offerings fashioned as faces 
 on the ancient pillars of Kota temples. Conceived as a time capsule addre
 ssed to Kota people 100 years in the future and narrated in Kota by Wolf'
 s longtime collaborator Duryodana\, the film explores music alongside tra
 nsformations of materials\, bodies\, and spiritual entities. It moves fro
 m pottery and musical-instrument making to their roles in mortuary ritual
 \, combining Wolf's footage from across decades with David G. Mandelbaum'
 s archival film and cylinder recordings from 1938 to create a layered por
 trait of Kota village life\, historical change\, and ethnographic encount
 er. [81 min\; documentary\; Kota and Tamil with English subtitles]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This screening is a public rental prese
 nted by the Journal for Audiovisual Ethnomusicology (JAVEM)\, a peer-revi
 ewed journal dedicated to audiovisual scholarship in ethnomusicology and 
 related fields. Together\, the three films demonstrate the range of audio
 visual ethnomusicology across generations: from Hugo Zemp's pioneering us
 e of film as a tool for musical analysis\, to Richard K. Wolf's longitudi
 nal and collaborative engagement with Kota musical and ritual life\, to M
 atthew Machin-Autenrieth and Dario Ranocchiari's exploration of music\, h
 istory\, and cultural memory in contemporary Granada.</p><p><i><strong>Th
 e Song of Harmonics</strong></i><strong>/</strong><i><strong>Le chant des
  harmoniques</strong></i><strong>(dir. Hugo Zemp\, 1989\, France):</stron
 g> Made with ethnomusicologist and musician Trần Quang Hải\, Hugo Zem
 p's landmark film investigates overtone\, or biphonic\, singing\, a techn
 ique in which a single singer produces a low fundamental while selectivel
 y emphasizing its harmonics to create a second audible melody. Moving bet
 ween instruction\, Mongolian khöömii performance\, and scientific inves
 tigation\, the film uses spectral and physiological imaging to make other
 wise invisible aspects of vocal production visible. <i>The Song of Harmon
 ics</i> is also an important example of ethnomusicological filmmaking in 
 which audiovisual media function not simply to document musical practice\
 , but as a method of research and analysis. This screening is presented i
 n tribute to Hugo Zemp (1937–2026)\, a foundational figure in visual et
 hnomusicology\, who passed away on January 3\, 2026. [38 min\; documentar
 y\; English and Mongolian]</p><p><i><strong>Resonant Histories: Echoes of
  Al-Andalus in Granada</strong></i><strong>(dir. Matthew Machin-Autenriet
 h and Dario Ranocchiari\, 2026\, Spain):</strong><i>Resonant Histories</i
 > traces the complex relationship between Arab-Andalusian music and flame
 nco in Granada\, a city where encounters between North African and Spanis
 h musicians have long shaped musical life. Traveling through the streets 
 of Sacromonte\, closely associated with flamenco\, and the Albaicín\, Gr
 anada's historic Arab quarter\, the filmmakers draw on musicians\, musico
 logists\, historians\, and local enthusiasts to explore the ways the city
 's past continues to resonate in the present. The film moves from colonia
 l exhibitions and early theatrical experiments combining Andalusí music 
 and flamenco to Granada's contemporary music scene\, asking how histories
  of encounter\, exchange\, and representation are remembered—and contin
 ually remade—through musical practice. [59 min\; documentary\; Spanish 
 with English subtitles]</p><p><i><strong>Pots of Millet\, Faces of Gold: 
 Transformation</strong></i><strong>(dir. Richard K. Wolf\, 2025\, India a
 nd USA):</strong> The first in a series of experimental ethnographic film
 s drawing on Richard K. Wolf's 35 years of research with the Kota people 
 of South India's Nilgiri Hills\, <i>Transformation</i> explores a communi
 ty of fewer than 2\,500 people whose history has included disease\, force
 d migration\, and disruptions in the transmission of traditional knowledg
 e. The film's title evokes two poles of Kota ritual life: millet\, centra
 l to multiday mortuary ceremonies\, and gold-colored offerings fashioned 
 as faces on the ancient pillars of Kota temples. Conceived as a time caps
 ule addressed to Kota people 100 years in the future and narrated in Kota
  by Wolf's longtime collaborator Duryodana\, the film explores music alon
 gside transformations of materials\, bodies\, and spiritual entities. It 
 moves from pottery and musical-instrument making to their roles in mortua
 ry ritual\, combining Wolf's footage from across decades with David G. Ma
 ndelbaum's archival film and cylinder recordings from 1938 to create a la
 yered portrait of Kota village life\, historical change\, and ethnographi
 c encounter. [81 min\; documentary\; Kota and Tamil with English subtitle
 s]</p>
