SUMMARY:Aanikoobijigan w/Q&A | Additional Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:"What's the difference between an archaeologist and a grave r
 obber?" an elder asks. "A degree\," he laughs. Aanikoobijigan examines th
 e ongoing struggle to repatriate Indigenous ancestors' remains from museu
 ms and archives\, exposing the thin line between archaeology and grave ro
 bbery. With striking visuals and grounded Indigenous perspectives\, Aanik
 oobijigan challenges institutional resistance to decolonization and affir
 ms that returning ancestors to the earth restores memory\, dignity\, and 
 balance\, reminding us that no community can be whole until their ancesto
 rs are finally laid to rest. The film's directors\, Adam and Zack Khalil\
 , were recently named among the cohort of Filmmaker Magazine's 25 New Fil
 m Faces to Watch. [80 min\; documentary\; English and Anishinaabemowin wi
 th English subtitles]The screening will be followed by a conversation bet
 weenIU Indianapolis professor Dr. Holly Cusack-McVeigh\, Anthropology and
  Museum Studies and Public Scholar of Collections and Community Curation\
 , who is also featured in the filmDr. Olof Olafardottir-Hamilton\, Assist
 ant Director\, Office of the Native American Graves Protection and Repatr
 iation Act\, Indiana UniversityWilliam Johnson\, Curator/Operations Manag
 er at the Saginaw Chippewa Tribe of Michigan's Ziibiwing Center of Anishi
 naabe Culture and Lifeways and Chairman of the Michigan Anishinaabek Cult
 ural Preservation &amp\; Repatriation Alliance (MACPRA)\, who is also fea
 tured in the film
