SUMMARY:Vindication Swim w/Q&A | Additional Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:Vindication Swim tells the remarkable story of Mercedes Gleit
 ze\, who in 1927 became the first British woman to swim the English Chann
 el. Facing press hostility\, anti-immigrant bias\, and constraints on wom
 en in sport\, Mercedes' achievement remains a powerful testament to her g
 rit\, courage\, and determination. Writer-director Elliott Hasler—only 
 23 when the film premiered—pursued period authenticity through grassroo
 ts collaboration with coastal communities throughout the UK. Three years 
 in the making\, the film's swimming sequences were shot miles out in the 
 Channel\, vividly capturing the perils of Mercedes' epic crossing. [98 mi
 n\; drama\; English]A Q&amp\;A with director Elliott Hasler and Radhika P
 arameswaran (Herman B Wells Endowed Professor\, Media School) will follow
  the screening.  Co-sponsored by the IU School of Public Health–Bloomin
 gton and The Media School.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><i>Vindication Swim</i> tells the remarka
 ble story of Mercedes Gleitze\, who in 1927 became the first British woma
 n to swim the English Channel. Facing press hostility\, anti-immigrant bi
 as\, and constraints on women in sport\, Mercedes' achievement remains a 
 powerful testament to her grit\, courage\, and determination. Writer-dire
 ctor Elliott Hasler—only 23 when the film premiered—pursued period au
 thenticity through grassroots collaboration with coastal communities thro
 ughout the UK. Three years in the making\, the film's swimming sequences 
 were shot miles out in the Channel\, vividly capturing the perils of Merc
 edes' epic crossing. [98 min\; drama\; English]</p><p><i><strong>A Q&amp\
 ;A with director Elliott Hasler and Radhika Parameswaran (Herman B Wells 
 Endowed Professor\, Media School) will follow the screening.</strong></i>
 </p><p>  Co-sponsored by the IU School of Public Health–Bloomington and
  The Media School.</p>
